## 6. Deliberately not doing

| Deferred item | Why deferral is safe |
| --- | --- |
| CI/CD | Local `make test` is enough for Phase 0; CI would add workflow polish before the template proves value. |
| Docker for the template | The local Python lifecycle should work directly with `uv`; Docker can wait until deployment packaging matters. |
| Docker Compose | No service stack is needed beyond local MLflow file tracking. |
| Pre-commit | Useful later, but not required to prove train/evaluate/test lifecycle. |
| Ruff / Black / mypy | Code quality tooling can be added after the skeleton is functional; Phase 0 is not style infrastructure. |
| `pytest-cov` | No Phase 0 gate asserts coverage, so coverage tooling is unused dependency weight. |
| Docs site | README plus this Obsidian note is enough. A docs site is polish. |
| Cookiecutter or Copier templating | A normal repo skeleton is sufficient; generator tooling is premature. |
| Model registry | Local MLflow run tracking is enough; registry discipline can wait until models become meaningful. |
| Remote MLflow server | Local `./mlruns` is the minimum viable tracking layer. |
| Database-backed MLflow | SQLite/Postgres-backed tracking is not needed for the smoke lifecycle. |
| ONNX export | Phase 1 owns ONNX export and the Go inference boundary. |
| Go service scaffold | Phase 0 is Python/infra setup. Go becomes active when there is a real model artifact worth serving. |
| `serving/schema.py` | Cut because it prejudges a Phase 1/ONNX boundary before metadata exists. Keep only an empty `serving/` package. |
| Real dataset ingestion | Phase 1 begins with UCI Bank Marketing. Phase 0 uses deterministic synthetic data only. |
| Real model training | Logistic smoke training only proves lifecycle mechanics. |
| Hyperparameter tuning | There is no meaningful model selection problem in Phase 0. |
| `evaluation_summary.md` | Cut because `metrics.json` and `predictions.csv` mechanically prove evaluation. A generated prose report is polish. |
| Experiment dashboards beyond MLflow UI | MLflow's local UI is enough. |
| GPU performance tuning | Phase 0 only verifies create-benchmark-pull-upload-destroy. Serious serving benchmarks belong later. |
| Multi-provider GPU support | DigitalOcean is the committed provider; RunPod fallback logic would reopen a locked decision. |
| Terraform | Deferred because Phase 0 compute is disposable and task-oriented; `doctl` plus strict teardown is the smaller correct tool. |
| Kubernetes | No Phase 0 workload needs orchestration. |
| Secrets manager integration | `.env` plus scoped tokens is enough for Phase 0; managed secrets can wait until repeated operational use. |
| Per-run token minting | Manual expiring-token rotation is enough; minting tokens per run adds API surface and failure modes. |
| Droplet-side Spaces upload | Artifacts are pulled locally and uploaded from the local machine so Spaces credentials never land on the droplet. |
| `Persistent=true` timer setting | Cut because the retry behavior comes from `OnUnitActiveSec=300`; the gate should assert the mechanism that actually matters. |
| Bootstrap verification inside the short-TTL self-destruct test | Cut because that test simulates lost local control. Bootstrap readiness is already verified by normal `gpu-run` and default `gpu-up`. |
| Embedding `gpu_benchmark.py` in cloud-init | Cut because cloud-init should stay minimal; the benchmark script is copied by `scp` after SSH readiness. |
| Published Cajun Data technical note | Publication happens at phase boundaries only if the output passes the publication filter; Phase 0 is primarily internal infrastructure. |

